  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "The loss is exactly (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.5 times the loss as a surface with contours. It is steeper along x, so steps overshoot along x once the learning rate passes 1/3 and diverge past 2/3.",
    "Noise is Gaussian around the true slope with spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "The orange arrow is the next step (−learning rate × noisy slope), the way the ball moves. The grey arrow is −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn at true length (scale 1).",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 8 (diverged). Off-bowl positions and heights are capped on screen. The Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. Each new run uses the next noise values. Reset replays the sequence from the first run and clears both paths.",
    "When running, the ball takes 5 steps per second at speed 1. Changing a setting mid-run affects the next steps only."
  ]
}